.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` and `aid` are written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.\n603:- [x] **`ard init` and `ard setup` (#303).** Decided 2026-11-24: `init` owns the content of `.ard/` (local tree by default, `--pr` for the `ard-init` PR, upgrades tracked in the user-editable `.ard/ard-init.lock`), and `setup` applies the local `.ard/` to GitHub, writing only `ard:*` labels and `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` and only checking rulesets, the bypass list, merge settings, App permissions and the bot-commits workflow. See the two paragraphs under Configuration. Implemented by #344 to #349; #350 adds `--create-gh-app`.\npackage initcmd\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"io/fs\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"net/url\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"reflect\"\n\t\"slices\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"testing/fstest\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/cli\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/configcmd\"\n\t\"